#!/usr/bin/env python3
# mac/usage/mac-usage - what the Mac Studio does, and what that would have
# cost on hosted Macs. Data to decide whether it pays its way.
#
#   mac-usage sample          root, every minute (launchd daemon): one host
#                             snapshot, appended to /var/log/mac-usage
#   mac-usage collect         worker, every 15 minutes (launchd agent): CI jobs
#                             from GitHub, agent sessions from Claude Code's
#                             transcripts, and Xcode's build and test logs,
#                             then writes the summary
#   mac-usage report [--since YYYY-MM-DD] [--json]
#                             anyone: prints the summary
#
# What each measures, and its limits (docs/mac-studio.md, "Usage and cost"):
# - CI: every job of every cut-watch repo from GitHub's Actions API, with the
#   runner it ran on. Minutes are rounded up per job, as GitHub bills them,
#   and a job that never reached a runner has none. Queue wait is the job's
#   start minus its creation. The first collect backfills from HISTORY_FROM.
#   Each Mac Studio job is priced at what the same job took on xcode-27 with
#   the same outcome, from that history (see hosted_durations), and counted
#   apart when GitHub would have run it anyway.
# - Agent sessions: worker's ~/.claude/projects/*/*.jsonl, with each
#   session's subagent transcripts (<session>/subagents/*.jsonl) counted as
#   part of it, and one session per worktree (session_id_of). A session is
#   active between two entries no more than IDLE_GAP_SECONDS apart, while
#   its Xcode processes run, and during its builds and test runs. Kept from
#   the transcripts: timestamps, the worktree, and counts of prompts, tool
#   calls, and calls that run Xcode or a simulator; a command is read only to
#   count it, and no text is kept. Builds and test runs come from Xcode's
#   LogStoreManifest.plist in the session's DerivedData: start, length and
#   status only. Sessions on MAC_SESSION_SERVERS that used Xcode or a
#   simulator are priced at a hosted Mac, as a range: one Mac whenever any
#   of them is active, up to one per session.
# - Host: load, CPU, memory, swap and swap writes, memory pressure and free
#   memory percentage, free disk, booted simulators, and the CPU and memory of
#   the VMs, Claude, Xcode and the simulators, from ps, top, vm_stat and
#   sysctl. Memory by group is top's physical footprint (parse_top_mem), not
#   ps's resident size, which counts shared pages in every process. Each
#   session's Xcode processes and booted simulators, and their CPU
#   (attribute_sessions). Power and thermal pressure from powermetrics: CPU,
#   GPU and ANE power only, so a floor under the power at the wall. No history before the
#   daemon first ran. Each is a reading once a minute, so a spike shorter
#   than that can fall between two; swap writes are a counter, so they miss
#   none, and a restart (kern.boottime) is counted from the counter's reset.
# - Headroom: whether 64 GB and 1 TB are enough, from the host samples, with
#   the MEM_ and DISK_ thresholds below.
#
# The prices and the purchase are constants, not settings, for the same reason
# as monitoring/report.go's thresholds: they are the judgement the summary
# exists to apply. Check them when they change.
#
# Python 3 standard library only. Nothing here sends data anywhere but
# GitHub's API, which it only reads.

